            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary meaning of 'the way' is Jesus Christ Himself as the exclusive path to God, with secondary meanings including the early Christian movement and the moral path of righteous conduct (the 'two ways').\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The symbol represents covenant conduct or one's manner of living ('the walk'), contrasting with the common view that it primarily represents Jesus Himself.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Jesus Christ (as the path to the Father) and the Christian movement.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Covenant conduct (the walk).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the person of Jesus Christ and the early Christian movement from the core definition of 'the way', restricting the symbol entirely to ethical behavior and covenant obedience.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies the primary referent of 'the way' as a person (Christ) and a resulting movement; the book restricts the referent to an action or manner of living (conduct/the walk), explicitly listing the Christological interpretation as a 'Commonly taught' view that it replaces.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By defining 'the way' strictly as 'covenant conduct' and relegating 'Jesus Himself' to the 'Commonly taught' note, the book diverges from the primary Christological consensus. It elevates a secondary consensus meaning (the moral/wisdom tradition of 'the way') to the sole definition of the symbol.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In the New Testament, particularly in John 14:6 ('I am the way') and throughout Acts (where the church is called 'the Way'), the term functions as a Christological title and a proper noun for the movement, meaning it cannot be fully reduced to 'covenant conduct' without ignoring these prominent usages.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
